  1. Target Code built with Covered Libraries.

  You may convey Target Code that combines Independent Modules with
unmodified Covered Libraries, under terms of your choice for the
Independent Modules, without complying with sections 4(d) and 4(e) of
the LGPL for the Covered Libraries it contains, provided that:
  (a) as to the Covered Libraries, you comply with sections 4(a), 4(b)
      and 4(c) of the LGPL and with its other terms except 4(d) and 4(e),
      including section 4's condition that your terms not restrict
      modification of the Covered Libraries' portions of the work and
      reverse engineering for debugging such modifications; and
  (b) you meet, for the Independent Modules, the terms of the licences
      that apply to them.
An Eligible Compilation Process does not make a Covered Library a
modified version. This Exception does not relax sections 4(b) and 4(c)
of the LGPL, including for Target Code in a single file or for a mobile
or embedded device, and does not say how they are met there.

  This section does not apply to a modified Covered Library, which stays
under the LGPL alone, nor to any Native Library, however Target Code
uses it. A Native Library stays under the LGPL alone, section 4(d) and
4(e) included: an application that loads it at run time as a shared
library the user can replace takes the route of section 4(d)(1); one that
links it into its executable takes the route of section 4(d)(0).

  2. Code generated from the Options File.

  Code that a Protocol Buffers compiler (protoc, any of its plugins, or a
build tool that runs them) generates from the Options File of a Release,
or from a copy of it changed only in comments, formatting or the
file-level options that name the generated code, and object code
compiled from that code, may be used, modified and conveyed under terms
of your choice. The same holds for anything that code generated from
your own definitions takes from the Options File when they import it and
use the options it declares.

  3. Output of the Generator.

  The files the Generator writes from your definitions, including the
parts of them it copies or adapts from its own source, may be used,
modified and conveyed under terms of your choice, within these limits:
  (a) this permission covers material of Fletcher in those files only to
      the extent that the Generator of a Release, unmodified, writes the
      same material from the same definitions and options;
  (b) it does not cover a file that is itself a code generator using that
      material as its template, or a file that reproduces any part of a
      Covered Library, a Native Library or another library of Fletcher,
      beyond the names, declarations and calls through which generated
      code uses that library's interface; and
  (c) it does not extend to the libraries the generated files use when
      they run. For C#, those are the Covered Libraries, under section 1.
      For C++ and TypeScript, they are Fletcher's C++ libraries and the
      package @eiva/fletcher-gateway-client, which stay under the LGPL
      alone: an application that links them statically or bundles them
      must meet sections 4(d) and 4(e) of the LGPL for them.

